turnkey-lamp-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The overlay no longer ships /etc/apt/sources.list.d/keel.sources
    (apt.keellinux.org, disabled) or /etc/apt/preferences.d/keel (the Keel
    origin at 1001). At 1001 apt downgraded every package newer than the
    archive's (tracker#23), and both files, at the paths common uses, would
    override the source and the 990 pin Keel-Linux/common#30 ships. The
    build time archive still wins over TurnKey's 999 pin during the build:
    conf.d/main pins it by its Label, l=Keel Linux staging, at 1001 before
    the upgrade and removes that pin with the build time source. The build
    then fails if any apt source or pin of the image still names the build
    time archive, in place of the check that keel.sources was disabled.
    tests/apt-files.bats checks the recipe.

  * LAMP is rebuilt as a child of the apache-php layer with MariaDB carried
    as a fab unit, instead of a single recipe on core that installs the web
    half itself. Decision 0013 is the reason: a database is a component, so
    LAMP and LAPP share one web layer rather than each being the child of
    its own database. The web half is no longer built twice.

        git clone --branch v1.0.0 \
            https://github.com/keel-linux/unit-mariadb.git unit.d/mariadb
        bt-layer lamp --parent apache-php

  * Two artefacts come out of this one recipe, split by content and not by
    topology, and the names say what is in them:

        lamp          Apache, PHP and a local MariaDB server
        lamp-client   the same stack with the client and the drivers and no
                      local server, for the database that lives elsewhere

    They differ by exactly one thing in the product directory: whether
    unit.d/mariadb is there. Nothing in the recipe asks which artefact is
    being built by name; conf.d/main reads what dpkg says the image
    contains, so an artefact that shipped a server it was not meant to have
    would fail its own build.

  * The appliance's own landing page, /var/www/index.php, carries the mark
    from docs/brand of the handbook. It is the appliance's page and not the
    operator's site, which is the one place the brand manual asks for the
    mark to stay until it is replaced, and the page says which file to
    replace. The host name it prints comes from the request and is escaped
    before it reaches the document, which the upstream page did not do.

  * The database account is the shared tree's: conf/adminer-mysql creates
    'adminer' with a build time password and
    firstboot.d/35adminer-mysqlpass replaces it at first boot from
    secrets.db_password, which the declarative reader renders as DB_PASS.
    Nothing new was written for it, and the layer is published with an
    account nobody can use until an instance description says otherwise.

  * The server answers on ::1 and on 127.0.0.1, as literal addresses.
    Debian's own default binds 127.0.0.1 alone and "localhost" is not an
    IPv6 name on Debian, so an appliance reaching its database over IPv6
    would find nothing listening.

  * Four packages upstream ships are deliberately not here, because each
    would make this recipe differ from LAPP in something other than the
    database, and the composition is being measured on exactly that:

        libapache2-mod-python   absent from Debian 13, unmaintained for
                                Apache 2.4 long before that
        php-xdebug              a step debugger and profiler, which belongs
                                on a developer's machine rather than
                                switched on in a published appliance
        php-pear                superseded by composer, which the parent
                                layer already carries
        python3-pygresql        LAPP's second Python driver; one driver per
                                language per stack

    Upstream's conf-available/remove-upgrade-header.conf is also not here.
    It is a web server setting with no database in it, so it belongs in the
    apache-php layer where both stacks would get it, and it is proposed
    there rather than kept in one stack.

